1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,136
Here is a hospital database, one row per
patient.

2
00:00:03,456 --> 00:00:08,043
A researcher asks an innocent question: how
many patients have condition X?

3
00:00:08,363 --> 00:00:10,411
The hospital releases no records.

4
00:00:10,731 --> 00:00:12,331
It just says: forty-one.

5
00:00:12,567 --> 00:00:15,490
A week later, one new patient, Alice, is
admitted.

6
00:00:15,810 --> 00:00:17,282
The researcher asks again.

7
00:00:17,602 --> 00:00:19,287
The answer is now forty-two.

8
00:00:19,533 --> 00:00:23,761
Subtract, and the researcher has learned
something very specific about Alice,

9
00:00:23,761 --> 00:00:25,464
without seeing a single record.

10
00:00:25,784 --> 00:00:28,173
Before we go on, try to fix this yourself.

11
00:00:28,493 --> 00:00:32,717
Pause and ponder: would rounding the count
to the nearest ten protect Alice?

12
00:00:33,037 --> 00:00:35,064
Pause the video if you need more time.

13
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:46,473
Most weeks, yes.

14
00:00:46,793 --> 00:00:50,059
But if the count goes from forty-four to
forty-five,

15
00:00:50,059 --> 00:00:54,644
the rounded answer jumps from forty to
fifty, and Alice is exposed again.

16
00:00:54,964 --> 00:00:59,231
Any fixed rule that ever changes its answer
has a jump like that somewhere.

17
00:00:59,467 --> 00:01:02,710
So what would it even mean for a statistic
to be private?

18
00:01:03,030 --> 00:01:05,909
And if the fix is to add noise, how much is
enough?

19
00:01:06,150 --> 00:01:08,932
In 2006, Cynthia Dwork, Frank McSherry,

20
00:01:08,932 --> 00:01:13,640
Kobbi Nissim and Adam Smith answered both
questions in this paper:

21
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:17,777
Calibrating Noise to Sensitivity in Private
Data Analysis.

22
00:01:18,097 --> 00:01:24,177
It founded what we now call differential
privacy, and in 2017 it won the Gödel Prize.

23
00:01:24,417 --> 00:01:28,499
This video covers its three big ideas: a
definition of privacy,

24
00:01:28,499 --> 00:01:33,100
a number called sensitivity, and a recipe
for how much noise is enough.

25
00:01:33,420 --> 00:01:37,106
Plus a surprising limit on what one
published table can achieve,

26
00:01:37,106 --> 00:01:39,179
if it must be private in this sense.

27
00:01:40,617 --> 00:01:42,921
First, a map of where this paper comes from.

28
00:01:43,241 --> 00:01:49,278
In 1965, Stanley Warner proposed randomized
response for sensitive surveys.

29
00:01:49,598 --> 00:01:52,329
In a popular version, you flip a coin in
private.

30
00:01:52,649 --> 00:01:54,206
Heads, you answer truthfully.

31
00:01:54,526 --> 00:01:58,003
Tails, you flip again and say yes for heads,
no for tails.

32
00:01:58,323 --> 00:02:00,612
Any single yes could be the coin talking,

33
00:02:00,612 --> 00:02:04,297
yet over thousands of people the true rate
can still be estimated.

34
00:02:04,617 --> 00:02:06,004
Keep this coin in mind.

35
00:02:06,250 --> 00:02:11,588
Over the following decades, statisticians
and computer scientists refined such tricks,

36
00:02:11,588 --> 00:02:16,554
in two flavours: scramble the data going in,
or scramble the answers coming out.

37
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:20,576
Meanwhile, the obvious fix, just remove the
names, kept failing.

38
00:02:20,896 --> 00:02:23,552
Latanya Sweeney showed that ZIP code,

39
00:02:23,552 --> 00:02:27,213
birth date and sex alone single out most
Americans,

40
00:02:27,213 --> 00:02:32,381
and in 1997 she linked supposedly anonymous
hospital records to a public

41
00:02:32,381 --> 00:02:36,042
voter list and found the governor of
Massachusetts.

42
00:02:36,362 --> 00:02:39,647
The lesson: privacy must be a property of
the process,

43
00:02:39,647 --> 00:02:44,939
not of how the released table looks, and it
must hold whatever else the attacker knows.

44
00:02:45,184 --> 00:02:50,048
Then, in 2003, Irit Dinur and Kobbi Nissim
proved something sobering.

45
00:02:50,368 --> 00:02:52,813
Answer too many questions too accurately,

46
00:02:52,813 --> 00:02:55,855
with errors much smaller than the square
root of n,

47
00:02:55,855 --> 00:02:59,136
and an attacker can rebuild almost the
entire database.

48
00:02:59,456 --> 00:03:02,826
Our subtraction was the two-question version
of this attack.

49
00:03:03,146 --> 00:03:05,685
Noise is the price of answering many
questions.

50
00:03:05,934 --> 00:03:10,734
The hopeful flip side: with a limited number
of questions, modest noise is enough.

51
00:03:11,054 --> 00:03:15,221
Dwork, Nissim and colleagues built this into
a framework called SuLQ,

52
00:03:15,221 --> 00:03:18,904
for sub-linear queries, which answers noisy
sums like counts.

53
00:03:19,224 --> 00:03:20,974
Remember that limit on questions.

54
00:03:21,294 --> 00:03:23,192
This paper turns it into a budget.

55
00:03:23,434 --> 00:03:25,188
But SuLQ only covered sums,

56
00:03:25,188 --> 00:03:29,023
and its definition tolerated a tiny chance
of a large leak.

57
00:03:29,343 --> 00:03:32,466
This paper takes the leap: any function of
the data,

58
00:03:32,466 --> 00:03:35,828
one clean definition, and one simple rule
for the noise.

59
00:03:36,148 --> 00:03:39,188
Fittingly, it appeared at a cryptography
conference,

60
00:03:39,188 --> 00:03:43,630
where the habit is to define security
against every possible attacker first,

61
00:03:43,630 --> 00:03:44,682
and then prove it.

62
00:03:47,033 --> 00:03:48,164
Here is the setting.

63
00:03:48,484 --> 00:03:53,604
A trusted server, the curator, holds a
database x: n rows, one per person.

64
00:03:53,924 --> 00:03:55,589
An analyst sends a query f,

65
00:03:55,589 --> 00:04:00,089
any function of the database that returns a
number, or a list of numbers.

66
00:04:00,333 --> 00:04:03,747
The curator never releases the honest
answer, f of x.

67
00:04:04,067 --> 00:04:08,675
Instead it draws random noise, Y, and
releases f of x plus Y.

68
00:04:08,995 --> 00:04:13,048
Because the analyst can keep asking, this is
called the interactive setting.

69
00:04:13,368 --> 00:04:18,659
The alternative, publishing one sanitized
table and walking away, is non-interactive.

70
00:04:18,979 --> 00:04:22,840
At the end, the paper proves the first is
fundamentally more powerful.

71
00:04:23,083 --> 00:04:27,521
So everything hinges on one question: what
distribution should Y have?

72
00:04:27,841 --> 00:04:30,166
Too little noise, and Alice is exposed.

73
00:04:30,486 --> 00:04:32,449
Too much, and the answer is useless.

74
00:04:32,769 --> 00:04:36,673
The title gives the answer: calibrate the
noise to the sensitivity.

75
00:04:36,993 --> 00:04:39,382
But first, what does private actually mean?

76
00:04:40,717 --> 00:04:42,807
The key move is to imagine two worlds.

77
00:04:43,127 --> 00:04:44,983
In one, the database is x.

78
00:04:45,303 --> 00:04:48,951
In the other, it is x prime: identical
except for one row.

79
00:04:49,271 --> 00:04:50,317
Say, Alice's.

80
00:04:50,637 --> 00:04:52,962
Databases like this are called neighbors.

81
00:04:53,282 --> 00:04:57,741
In the hospital, Alice was added; here, her
row just says something different.

82
00:04:58,061 --> 00:05:01,048
Either way, no single person's row should
matter much.

83
00:05:01,284 --> 00:05:05,934
Run the mechanism, the curator's randomized
answering rule, in each world.

84
00:05:06,254 --> 00:05:08,344
Whatever the analyst gets to see,

85
00:05:08,344 --> 00:05:12,334
the paper calls the transcript; for now, a
single noisy answer.

86
00:05:12,654 --> 00:05:14,588
Because the mechanism is random,

87
00:05:14,588 --> 00:05:18,094
each world gives a whole distribution of
possible outputs.

88
00:05:18,334 --> 00:05:21,811
Pick any output t, and compare the heights
of the two curves there.

89
00:05:22,131 --> 00:05:24,478
Slide t along and watch their ratio.

90
00:05:24,798 --> 00:05:29,042
A private mechanism keeps that ratio close
to one everywhere:

91
00:05:29,042 --> 00:05:33,566
never above e to the epsilon, never below e
to the minus epsilon.

92
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:38,493
That is the paper's definition, which it
calls epsilon-indistinguishability.

93
00:05:38,813 --> 00:05:41,800
The log of the ratio is the privacy loss at
t.

94
00:05:42,120 --> 00:05:45,947
For every pair of neighbors, every analyst
and every output,

95
00:05:45,947 --> 00:05:48,818
it must be at most epsilon in absolute
value.

96
00:05:49,138 --> 00:05:51,553
The absolute value makes it symmetric:

97
00:05:51,553 --> 00:05:54,984
a ratio of two and a ratio of one half count
the same.

98
00:05:55,304 --> 00:05:59,592
For small epsilon, e to the epsilon is about
one plus epsilon.

99
00:05:59,834 --> 00:06:02,074
Let's test it on Warner's coin from our map.

100
00:06:02,394 --> 00:06:07,322
Pause and ponder: if Alice's true answer is
yes, how likely is she to say yes?

101
00:06:07,642 --> 00:06:08,730
And if it is no?

102
00:06:21,567 --> 00:06:25,116
If the truth is yes, she says yes three
quarters of the time:

103
00:06:25,116 --> 00:06:26,687
heads, or tails then heads.

104
00:06:27,007 --> 00:06:30,783
If it is no, only the second coin can say
yes: one quarter.

105
00:06:31,103 --> 00:06:35,158
The worst ratio is three, so the coin
satisfies the definition

106
00:06:35,158 --> 00:06:39,082
with epsilon equal to the log of three,
about one point one.

107
00:06:39,317 --> 00:06:42,474
Now be the attacker, trying to tell the two
worlds apart.

108
00:06:42,794 --> 00:06:47,282
By Bayes' rule, your new odds are your old
odds times exactly this ratio,

109
00:06:47,282 --> 00:06:52,138
so they move by at most a factor of e to the
epsilon, whatever you knew before.

110
00:06:52,458 --> 00:06:56,389
Starting from fifty-fifty, epsilon one tenth
leaves you at most

111
00:06:56,389 --> 00:07:00,757
fifty-two and a half percent sure; epsilon
one, at most seventy-three.

112
00:07:01,077 --> 00:07:04,618
Epsilon is set by policy; the paper calls it
the leakage.

113
00:07:04,867 --> 00:07:06,723
Notice what this does not promise.

114
00:07:07,043 --> 00:07:10,300
If a study reveals that smokers get more
heart disease,

115
00:07:10,300 --> 00:07:13,912
and Alice smokes, people may now think she
is at higher risk.

116
00:07:14,232 --> 00:07:16,111
That is not a privacy violation:

117
00:07:16,111 --> 00:07:19,577
the same lesson could be learned from
everyone else's data,

118
00:07:19,577 --> 00:07:23,747
so it would happen even if Alice's row were
replaced by someone else's.

119
00:07:25,183 --> 00:07:28,149
Why demand a ratio bound for every single
output?

120
00:07:28,469 --> 00:07:31,407
Cryptography often settles for something
weaker:

121
00:07:31,407 --> 00:07:35,018
statistical distance, half the area between
the two curves.

122
00:07:35,338 --> 00:07:39,903
Equivalently, the most the probability of
any event can differ between the worlds.

123
00:07:40,223 --> 00:07:42,612
The paper shows why that is not enough here.

124
00:07:42,850 --> 00:07:47,672
Consider this mechanism: pick one row at
random and publish it, word for word.

125
00:07:47,992 --> 00:07:53,048
Change one person's row, and the output
distribution moves by only one over n.

126
00:07:53,368 --> 00:07:57,826
For a big database that is tiny, so by the
averaged measure this looks private.

127
00:07:58,066 --> 00:08:00,605
But every output is somebody's complete
record.

128
00:08:00,925 --> 00:08:05,301
The ratio test catches it: the output
showing Alice's real value

129
00:08:05,301 --> 00:08:09,608
has probability one over n in one world, and
zero in the other.

130
00:08:09,928 --> 00:08:11,421
The ratio is infinite.

131
00:08:11,666 --> 00:08:16,744
Averages let catastrophes slip through, as
long as each is rare for any single person.

132
00:08:17,064 --> 00:08:19,560
A ratio bound rules them out everywhere.

133
00:08:19,880 --> 00:08:23,046
It also settles our rounding question: at
the jump,

134
00:08:23,046 --> 00:08:28,136
one world gives that answer with probability
one, the other with probability zero.

135
00:08:28,456 --> 00:08:31,442
To pass this test, a mechanism has to be
random.

136
00:08:32,883 --> 00:08:34,547
So how much noise do we need?

137
00:08:34,867 --> 00:08:38,869
It depends on a single property of the
query: its sensitivity,

138
00:08:38,869 --> 00:08:42,612
the most that changing one row can ever
change the answer,

139
00:08:42,612 --> 00:08:47,646
over all pairs of neighboring databases,
including ones that do not exist yet.

140
00:08:47,883 --> 00:08:50,660
For a counting query, like our hospital
count,

141
00:08:50,660 --> 00:08:53,195
one row changes the answer by at most one.

142
00:08:53,515 --> 00:08:54,752
Sensitivity one.

143
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:59,163
Now a histogram: split the possible values
of a row into d bins,

144
00:08:59,163 --> 00:09:01,699
and release how many rows fall in each.

145
00:09:02,019 --> 00:09:05,091
Before I show you, pause: if Alice's row
changes,

146
00:09:05,091 --> 00:09:08,163
how much can the whole histogram change in
total?

147
00:09:08,483 --> 00:09:10,338
Does it depend on the number of bins?

148
00:09:19,183 --> 00:09:21,871
She moves out of one bin and into another:

149
00:09:21,871 --> 00:09:25,135
one count goes down by one, another goes up
by one.

150
00:09:25,455 --> 00:09:26,863
Total change: two.

151
00:09:27,183 --> 00:09:31,646
Adding up absolute changes across
coordinates like this is the L1 norm,

152
00:09:31,646 --> 00:09:35,418
the paper's way to measure sensitivity for
lists of numbers.

153
00:09:35,738 --> 00:09:39,023
And it is two whether there are five bins or
five thousand.

154
00:09:39,343 --> 00:09:41,540
It does not depend on the dimension at all.

155
00:09:41,783 --> 00:09:45,602
Contrast that with: what is the largest
income in the database?

156
00:09:45,922 --> 00:09:48,723
One billionaire can move that answer by a
billion,

157
00:09:48,723 --> 00:09:51,021
and with no cap there is no limit at all.

158
00:09:51,341 --> 00:09:55,095
The standard fix is to cap every value, say
at a million, first.

159
00:09:55,415 --> 00:09:59,895
Remember that trick: it is exactly what
private deep learning does to gradients.

160
00:10:00,133 --> 00:10:01,456
Two things to remember.

161
00:10:01,776 --> 00:10:04,866
Sensitivity is a property of the function
alone,

162
00:10:04,866 --> 00:10:08,923
not of the particular database, and it is
not chosen by policy.

163
00:10:09,243 --> 00:10:10,651
Epsilon is a choice.

164
00:10:10,971 --> 00:10:12,528
Sensitivity is a fact.

165
00:10:13,967 --> 00:10:15,055
Now for the noise.

166
00:10:15,375 --> 00:10:19,471
The paper uses the Laplace distribution:
symmetric, sharply peaked,

167
00:10:19,471 --> 00:10:23,140
and falling off exponentially with distance
from the center.

168
00:10:23,460 --> 00:10:26,106
Its width is set by a scale parameter,
lambda.

169
00:10:27,150 --> 00:10:28,238
Here is the trick.

170
00:10:28,558 --> 00:10:33,705
Center one Laplace curve at f of x, forty-
one, and another at f of x prime,

171
00:10:33,705 --> 00:10:37,412
forty-two: the output distributions of our
two worlds.

172
00:10:37,650 --> 00:10:40,722
Now plot the log of their ratio for every
output t.

173
00:10:41,042 --> 00:10:43,858
To the left of forty-one, it is exactly
constant.

174
00:10:44,178 --> 00:10:46,460
To the right of forty-two, constant again.

175
00:10:46,780 --> 00:10:48,402
In between, a straight ramp.

176
00:10:48,722 --> 00:10:51,138
It never rises above one over lambda,

177
00:10:51,138 --> 00:10:56,168
and never falls below minus one over lambda:
the shift, divided by the scale.

178
00:10:56,817 --> 00:10:58,750
Why? On a log scale,

179
00:10:58,750 --> 00:11:03,835
a Laplace density is a tent whose sides have
slope one over lambda, never steeper.

180
00:11:04,155 --> 00:11:05,326
Slide the tent over,

181
00:11:05,326 --> 00:11:09,659
and the gap between the tents can never
exceed the slide times that slope.

182
00:11:09,979 --> 00:11:12,194
That is the whole design principle:

183
00:11:12,194 --> 00:11:15,548
privacy needs noise whose log density is
never steep.

184
00:11:16,284 --> 00:11:21,468
In symbols: two distances to t can differ by
at most the distance between the centers.

185
00:11:21,788 --> 00:11:26,609
So the privacy loss of any output is at most
the sensitivity divided by lambda.

186
00:11:26,929 --> 00:11:32,448
Set lambda to the sensitivity over epsilon,
and the ratio stays within e to the epsilon,

187
00:11:32,448 --> 00:11:35,270
for every output and every pair of
neighbors.

188
00:11:35,590 --> 00:11:37,489
Privacy, proven in one line.

189
00:11:37,734 --> 00:11:42,427
If the query returns several numbers, add
independent Laplace noise to each.

190
00:11:42,747 --> 00:11:46,160
The joint density then depends on the L1
distance,

191
00:11:46,160 --> 00:11:50,256
which is exactly why sensitivity is measured
in the L1 norm.

192
00:11:50,576 --> 00:11:52,161
That is Proposition 1:

193
00:11:52,161 --> 00:11:56,699
Laplace noise of scale S of f over epsilon
in every coordinate.

194
00:11:56,934 --> 00:11:59,695
Pause and ponder: why not something simpler,

195
00:11:59,695 --> 00:12:03,398
like uniform noise anywhere between minus
ten and plus ten?

196
00:12:14,234 --> 00:12:15,343
Look at the edges.

197
00:12:15,663 --> 00:12:20,601
An output of fifty-one and a half is
possible if the true count is forty-two,

198
00:12:20,601 --> 00:12:22,781
but impossible if it is forty-one:

199
00:12:22,781 --> 00:12:26,564
the same infinite ratio that sank the
random-row mechanism.

200
00:12:26,884 --> 00:12:29,167
Good noise must never rule an output out.

201
00:12:29,400 --> 00:12:31,320
And why not the familiar bell curve?

202
00:12:31,640 --> 00:12:35,288
On a log scale a Gaussian is a parabola,
steeper and steeper,

203
00:12:35,288 --> 00:12:38,936
so the gap between two shifted parabolas
grows without bound.

204
00:12:39,256 --> 00:12:43,117
In the tails the ratio explodes, and no
single epsilon works.

205
00:12:43,437 --> 00:12:46,243
Hold on to that Gaussian, though: it comes
back,

206
00:12:46,243 --> 00:12:50,862
and it is the noise you would actually use
to train a neural network privately.

207
00:12:51,100 --> 00:12:57,094
So here is the recipe in the title: noise
scale equals sensitivity divided by epsilon.

208
00:12:57,414 --> 00:12:59,974
A more sensitive question needs more noise.

209
00:13:00,294 --> 00:13:02,492
Stronger privacy needs more noise.

210
00:13:04,033 --> 00:13:08,471
Back to the hospital, with epsilon one half:
Laplace noise of scale two.

211
00:13:08,791 --> 00:13:12,289
Week one, the curator reports about forty-
three point seven.

212
00:13:12,609 --> 00:13:16,343
Week two, when the true count is forty-two,
about forty point six.

213
00:13:16,663 --> 00:13:20,652
Subtract as before, and Alice seems to have
lowered the count by three.

214
00:13:20,972 --> 00:13:25,537
A fifty-fifty guess about her can now move
to at most about sixty-two percent.

215
00:13:25,783 --> 00:13:30,114
Now look at what the noise scale depends on:
the sensitivity and epsilon.

216
00:13:30,434 --> 00:13:32,162
Not the size of the database.

217
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:37,800
With a hundred patients, noise of typical
size two is a few percent of the answer.

218
00:13:38,120 --> 00:13:40,254
With a million, it is a rounding error.

219
00:13:40,574 --> 00:13:45,348
Alice is protected by noise that is large
compared to her own contribution,

220
00:13:45,348 --> 00:13:48,658
which is one, but tiny compared to the
population's.

221
00:13:49,900 --> 00:13:51,522
Privacy for individuals.

222
00:13:51,842 --> 00:13:53,612
Accuracy for populations.

223
00:13:53,932 --> 00:13:55,105
That is the bargain.

224
00:13:55,350 --> 00:13:57,270
Here is one you can answer yourself.

225
00:13:57,590 --> 00:14:01,409
Pause and ponder: the noise has size about
one over epsilon.

226
00:14:01,729 --> 00:14:04,928
What goes wrong if epsilon is much smaller
than one over n?

227
00:14:17,766 --> 00:14:22,758
The noise outgrows n, bigger than the count
could ever be, and the answer is pure noise.

228
00:14:23,078 --> 00:14:24,828
This is not Laplace's fault.

229
00:14:25,148 --> 00:14:30,016
Any two databases are linked by a chain of
at most n single-row changes,

230
00:14:30,016 --> 00:14:33,802
each changing probabilities by at most e to
the epsilon,

231
00:14:33,802 --> 00:14:37,116
so the ends differ by at most e to the n
epsilon.

232
00:14:37,436 --> 00:14:42,214
If n epsilon is tiny, every database looks
alike, and nothing can be learned.

233
00:14:42,534 --> 00:14:46,630
This chain trick is called a hybrid
argument, and it comes back at the end.

234
00:14:48,067 --> 00:14:49,944
One question is never enough.

235
00:14:50,264 --> 00:14:55,363
Real analysts ask many, adaptively, each
chosen after seeing earlier answers.

236
00:14:55,683 --> 00:14:58,392
Maybe you spot a spike in one bin, and zoom
in.

237
00:14:58,634 --> 00:15:00,639
The paper's Theorem 1 handles this.

238
00:15:00,959 --> 00:15:05,140
Write the probability of the whole
transcript as a product, step by step.

239
00:15:05,460 --> 00:15:09,766
The analyst's choice of the next question is
the same in both worlds,

240
00:15:09,766 --> 00:15:13,823
so in the ratio it cancels, leaving one
Laplace ratio per answer.

241
00:15:14,143 --> 00:15:18,707
If you know reinforcement learning, this is
the trajectory-ratio trick:

242
00:15:18,707 --> 00:15:23,914
whatever is identical in both worlds
cancels, and the per-step log ratios add up.

243
00:15:24,150 --> 00:15:28,032
So privacy losses add up, and epsilon
behaves like a budget.

244
00:15:28,352 --> 00:15:32,299
Each answer spends part of it; once it is
spent, the curator stops.

245
00:15:32,619 --> 00:15:34,994
That is the answer to Dinur and Nissim:

246
00:15:34,994 --> 00:15:38,892
the budget makes the limit on questions
explicit and measurable.

247
00:15:40,434 --> 00:15:42,844
You can now compute a real saving yourself.

248
00:15:43,164 --> 00:15:46,791
Pause: a histogram with d bins, total budget
epsilon.

249
00:15:47,111 --> 00:15:51,207
Treat each bin as its own counting query and
split the budget evenly.

250
00:15:51,527 --> 00:15:53,404
How much noise does each bin get?

251
00:16:06,250 --> 00:16:10,922
Each bin gets one over d of the budget, so
noise of scale d over epsilon.

252
00:16:11,242 --> 00:16:14,052
Treated as one query with sensitivity two,

253
00:16:14,052 --> 00:16:17,130
each bin gets two over epsilon, whatever d
is.

254
00:16:17,450 --> 00:16:22,296
The earlier framework's sharper analysis got
this down to about the square root of d,

255
00:16:22,296 --> 00:16:23,722
but it still grew with d.

256
00:16:25,067 --> 00:16:27,435
Sensitivity reaches far beyond counts.

257
00:16:27,755 --> 00:16:29,526
Three examples from the paper.

258
00:16:29,834 --> 00:16:35,082
First, picture a social network where each
possible link is one row of the database.

259
00:16:35,402 --> 00:16:38,260
How many links must you cut to split the
network in two?

260
00:16:38,580 --> 00:16:43,743
That minimum cut moves by at most one when
one link changes, so it is one-sensitive.

261
00:16:44,063 --> 00:16:46,141
In general, any question of the form,

262
00:16:46,141 --> 00:16:50,804
how many rows would you have to change to
make something true, has sensitivity one.

263
00:16:51,050 --> 00:16:55,316
Second: if an algorithm that rarely looks at
any particular row,

264
00:16:55,316 --> 00:16:58,250
like one working from a small random sample,

265
00:16:58,250 --> 00:17:01,450
approximates f to within sigma most of the
time,

266
00:17:01,450 --> 00:17:05,450
on every database, then f has sensitivity at
most two sigma.

267
00:17:05,770 --> 00:17:06,858
That is Lemma 1.

268
00:17:07,100 --> 00:17:10,538
Third, the answer need not be a number: a
ranking, a set,

269
00:17:10,538 --> 00:17:14,097
a string of bits, anything with a distance
between answers.

270
00:17:14,417 --> 00:17:17,067
Pick an output with probability that decays

271
00:17:17,067 --> 00:17:20,333
exponentially with its distance from the
true answer,

272
00:17:20,333 --> 00:17:23,292
at a rate of epsilon over twice the
sensitivity.

273
00:17:23,612 --> 00:17:28,274
For bit strings, that flips each bit with
probability a little below one half:

274
00:17:28,274 --> 00:17:30,844
Warner's coin again, applied to the answer.

275
00:17:33,683 --> 00:17:35,411
Now the last part of the paper.

276
00:17:35,731 --> 00:17:40,671
Statisticians and data miners traditionally
prefer the non-interactive model:

277
00:17:40,671 --> 00:17:45,033
sanitize the data once, publish it, and let
anyone compute anything.

278
00:17:45,353 --> 00:17:47,208
Can that work under this definition?

279
00:17:47,450 --> 00:17:49,328
The paper proves a striking limit.

280
00:17:49,648 --> 00:17:51,568
Let each row be a string of d bits.

281
00:17:51,888 --> 00:17:54,704
Here is a family of simple counting queries:

282
00:17:54,704 --> 00:17:58,160
give each row its own mask, a subset of bit
positions,

283
00:17:58,160 --> 00:18:02,256
and count the rows with an odd number of
ones inside their mask.

284
00:18:02,576 --> 00:18:06,426
Each query has sensitivity one, so an
interactive curator can

285
00:18:06,426 --> 00:18:10,212
answer any one of them with noise of about
one over epsilon.

286
00:18:10,450 --> 00:18:13,736
But a one-shot release must prepare for all
of them at once.

287
00:18:14,056 --> 00:18:17,840
Theorem 3 shows that for at least two thirds
of these queries,

288
00:18:17,840 --> 00:18:20,648
the release looks almost the same for a
random

289
00:18:20,648 --> 00:18:24,249
database where every row has even parity,
true answer zero,

290
00:18:24,249 --> 00:18:27,240
as for one where every row is odd, true
answer n.

291
00:18:27,560 --> 00:18:31,304
The most extreme difference possible, and it
cannot be seen,

292
00:18:31,304 --> 00:18:34,549
unless the database is exponentially large:
roughly,

293
00:18:34,549 --> 00:18:38,418
every four extra bits per row doubles the
rows you would need.

294
00:18:38,666 --> 00:18:39,709
Pause and ponder:

295
00:18:39,709 --> 00:18:44,490
couldn't an analyst just ask the interactive
curator all of these queries too?

296
00:18:55,333 --> 00:18:57,040
No: the budget would run out.

297
00:18:57,360 --> 00:19:01,760
The curator only has to answer the few
questions someone actually asks,

298
00:19:01,760 --> 00:19:05,848
chosen later. A published release cannot
know which few those will be,

299
00:19:05,848 --> 00:19:08,176
so it must be ready for all of them at once.

300
00:19:08,496 --> 00:19:09,754
That is where it breaks.

301
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:11,878
The proof idea uses two facts.

302
00:19:12,198 --> 00:19:16,592
Privacy forces the release to treat every
possible row almost alike.

303
00:19:16,912 --> 00:19:21,474
And a random mask splits all rows into two
halves mixed like salt and pepper,

304
00:19:21,474 --> 00:19:24,614
so the even half is like a random poll of
everything.

305
00:19:24,934 --> 00:19:28,176
Then the chain trick: start from completely
random rows,

306
00:19:28,176 --> 00:19:30,608
and swap them one at a time for even ones.

307
00:19:30,928 --> 00:19:34,447
Each swap barely moves the output, and
unless n is huge,

308
00:19:34,447 --> 00:19:36,710
all n swaps together barely move it.

309
00:19:37,030 --> 00:19:41,381
The same goes for odd rows, so both
databases look like random rows.

310
00:19:41,616 --> 00:19:45,669
Warner's coin, randomized response, where
each person scrambles

311
00:19:45,669 --> 00:19:49,851
their own row so nobody holds the raw data,
is even more limited.

312
00:19:50,171 --> 00:19:54,083
Proposition 2: even when every row uses the
same mask,

313
00:19:54,083 --> 00:19:59,878
for most masks it cannot estimate the odd
count unless n is exponentially large.

314
00:20:00,116 --> 00:20:01,695
Careful with the quantifiers.

315
00:20:02,015 --> 00:20:06,324
For any one query known in advance, a one-
shot release can answer it well.

316
00:20:06,644 --> 00:20:10,544
What is impossible, unless the database is
exponentially large,

317
00:20:10,544 --> 00:20:13,578
is one private release that answers most of
them.

318
00:20:13,898 --> 00:20:17,744
The lesson: for broad, flexible accuracy
with strong privacy,

319
00:20:17,744 --> 00:20:19,572
keep the curator in the loop.

320
00:20:21,217 --> 00:20:23,755
Back to our map, to see what grew from this
paper.

321
00:20:24,075 --> 00:20:28,300
The same year, in an invited paper titled
simply Differential Privacy,

322
00:20:28,300 --> 00:20:31,137
Dwork introduced the name the field still
uses.

323
00:20:31,457 --> 00:20:34,935
Another 2006 paper, with Kenthapadi,
McSherry,

324
00:20:34,935 --> 00:20:38,262
Mironov and Naor, added a tiny slack, delta.

325
00:20:38,582 --> 00:20:42,102
Remember the Gaussian whose ratio escaped
the band in the tails?

326
00:20:42,422 --> 00:20:46,198
Delta pays for those rare tails, and lets
the bell curve back in.

327
00:20:46,434 --> 00:20:51,682
Scoring every possible answer became
McSherry and Talwar's exponential mechanism.

328
00:20:52,002 --> 00:20:55,556
The privacy budget grew into a theory of
composition,

329
00:20:55,556 --> 00:21:00,317
with a surprise from Dwork, Rothblum and
Vadhan: allow that tiny delta,

330
00:21:00,317 --> 00:21:05,079
and the total loss of k questions grows only
like the square root of k.

331
00:21:05,317 --> 00:21:09,320
Warner's coin, the model Section four showed
is most limited,

332
00:21:09,320 --> 00:21:12,405
is what we now call local differential
privacy,

333
00:21:12,405 --> 00:21:15,621
used by Google in Chrome and by Apple on
iPhones.

334
00:21:15,941 --> 00:21:17,263
Why the weakest model?

335
00:21:17,583 --> 00:21:21,932
Nobody has to be trusted with the raw data,
and with millions of users,

336
00:21:21,932 --> 00:21:24,688
a few simple statistics can afford the
noise.

337
00:21:24,934 --> 00:21:30,316
In 2016, Abadi and colleagues made it
practical to train deep networks privately:

338
00:21:30,316 --> 00:21:35,633
clip each example's gradient, like our
income cap, which bounds its sensitivity;

339
00:21:35,633 --> 00:21:39,952
add Gaussian noise; and track the budget
over thousands of steps.

340
00:21:40,272 --> 00:21:43,814
If you train models on patient data, this is
your entry point.

341
00:21:44,134 --> 00:21:48,224
A trained network is just another f of x,
and attacks that ask whether

342
00:21:48,224 --> 00:21:52,432
a patient was in the training set are our
subtraction attack, scaled up.

343
00:21:52,667 --> 00:21:54,341
And for the 2020 census,

344
00:21:54,341 --> 00:21:59,781
the US Census Bureau protected its published
tables with differential privacy;

345
00:21:59,781 --> 00:22:03,547
only a few counts, like state populations,
were exact.

346
00:22:03,867 --> 00:22:05,552
But that is a one-shot release.

347
00:22:05,872 --> 00:22:08,496
Pause and ponder: does Section four forbid
it?

348
00:22:17,334 --> 00:22:22,496
No. The Census tuned its release for a fixed
set of tables chosen in advance.

349
00:22:22,816 --> 00:22:25,303
Section four only rules out one release

350
00:22:25,303 --> 00:22:28,875
that is accurate for most of a huge family
of questions.

351
00:22:32,217 --> 00:22:37,337
Let's recap. One: privacy means changing any
one person's row changes

352
00:22:37,337 --> 00:22:42,137
the probability of any output by at most a
factor of e to the epsilon.

353
00:22:42,457 --> 00:22:46,702
Two: a query's sensitivity is the most one
row can change its answer.

354
00:22:47,022 --> 00:22:51,629
Three: Laplace noise with scale sensitivity
over epsilon makes it private,

355
00:22:51,629 --> 00:22:54,617
with error that does not grow with the
database.

356
00:22:54,937 --> 00:22:58,800
Four: one private published table cannot
answer most simple

357
00:22:58,800 --> 00:23:02,532
parity counts unless the database is
exponentially large.

358
00:23:03,167 --> 00:23:06,175
Some questions to test yourself; pause after
each.

359
00:23:06,495 --> 00:23:11,252
First: what is the sensitivity of the
average of n numbers between zero and one?

360
00:23:19,500 --> 00:23:24,350
Second: in a medical imaging dataset, each
patient contributes fifty slices,

361
00:23:24,350 --> 00:23:27,350
and each slice's gradient is clipped to size
C.

362
00:23:27,670 --> 00:23:30,658
How much can one patient change the summed
gradient?

363
00:23:40,900 --> 00:23:44,292
Third: why does the median give the Laplace
mechanism trouble?

364
00:23:44,612 --> 00:23:48,026
Answers, and more exercises, are in the
companion notes.

365
00:23:56,267 --> 00:24:00,170
Next time someone says a dataset is safe
because it is anonymized,

366
00:24:00,170 --> 00:24:02,240
you will know the better questions:

367
00:24:02,240 --> 00:24:05,611
what is the epsilon, and what counts as one
person's row?

